--TEST--
`Core\Sse::stream` answers a request with an event stream, and every event a program sends is on the wire as the client will read it — `rule:concurrency/a-stream-that-outlives-its-request-is-a-connection` and `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Door two is the stream that *ends* with its request, so off a connection
// there is no cell to open and the events reach this program's own output --
// which is what makes a whole event stream assertable here at all, no `.nvst`
// case being a request a server is answering. What is pinned is the order and
// the blank line between events: a member that buffered them and flushed at the
// end would still print plausibly against one event.
var $events = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
    $sse->send("ready");
    $sse->send("still here");
});
echo $events;
echo "--\n";

// The optional fields are their own lines and stand before the payload: the
// name is what a client dispatches on, and the id is what it echoes back in
// `Last-Event-ID` when it reconnects.
var $named = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
    $sse->send("sold", event: "order", id: "42");
});
echo $named;
echo "--\n";

// A `string` goes out as it was written and anything else is JSON-encoded by
// the encoder `Core\Json::encode` is, so a caller never has to choose between
// two members for one distinction it does not make.
var $encoded = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
    $sse->send({room: "lobby", waiting: 3});
    $sse->send(7);
});
echo $encoded;
echo "--\n";
--EXPECT--
data: ready

data: still here

--
event: order
id: 42
data: sold

--
data: {"room":"lobby","waiting":3}

data: 7

--
